---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<Base title="About" description="Who AVI is, how it works, and how it is funded.">
  <h1>About AVI</h1>

  <p>
    Founded in 2016, the Association of Vapers India (AVI) is now in its
    second decade as India's leading consumer advocacy voice for tobacco
    harm reduction (THR) — a group founded by former smokers who quit
    through vaping, working for the right of India's tobacco users to
    access lower-risk alternatives to combustible tobacco. Our focus has
    expanded to include smokeless nicotine products — nicotine pouches and
    snus — given that an estimated 200 million Indians use smokeless
    tobacco, roughly twice the number who smoke.
  </p>

  <p>
    AVI is not a lone effort. It's one of roughly 50 consumer advocacy
    organisations for safer nicotine products active worldwide, most of
    them volunteer-run on shoestring budgets, organised under regional
    coalitions — Europe, Africa, Latin America and Asia-Pacific each have
    one — that in turn sit under the International Network of Nicotine
    Consumer Organisations (INNCO), a global federation with UN observer
    status. AVI has represented Indian and Global South consumers at that
    level directly, including at the Global Forum on Nicotine and WHO
    Framework Convention on Tobacco Control conferences — work aimed at
    keeping India's actual experience, not just Western tobacco-control
    models, in front of the people who set global policy.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div><Icon name="scale" /><strong>8</strong><span>court cases fought since 2017</span></div>
    <div><Icon name="megaphone" /><strong>60+</strong><span>press features</span></div>
    <div><Icon name="chart" /><strong>10+</strong><span>years of advocacy</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Over ten years, AVI has built a track record few consumer bodies
    anywhere in the Global South can match: it has organised and fought
    litigation across more Indian courts than any other THR advocacy
    group, built a citizen movement that has taken its case directly to
    government and the public, and helped carry India's experience — good
    and bad — onto the international stage. See our
    <a href="/litigation">litigation</a> and
    <a href="/press">press coverage</a> for the documented record of this
    work, and our <a href="/testimonials">testimonials</a> page for
    accounts from the Indians this work has been for all along.
  </p>

